```rust
#[derive(Debug, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]
enum Edit {
    Match(char),
    Insert(char),
    Delete(char),
    Replace(char, char),
}

fn edit_distance_and_script(a: &str, b: &str) -> (usize, Vec<Edit>) {
    let a: Vec<char> = a.chars().collect();
    let b: Vec<char> = b.chars().collect();

    let n = a.len();
    let m = b.len();

    // dp[i][j] = edit distance between a[..i] and b[..j]
    let mut dp = vec![vec![0usize; m + 1]; n + 1];

    for i in 0..=n {
        dp[i][0] = i;
    }
    for j in 0..=m {
        dp[0][j] = j;
    }

    for i in 1..=n {
        for j in 1..=m {
            let cost = if a[i - 1] == b[j - 1] { 0 } else { 1 };

            dp[i][j] = (dp[i - 1][j - 1] + cost)
                .min(dp[i - 1][j] + 1)
                .min(dp[i][j - 1] + 1);
        }
    }

    // Traceback to build the edit script.
    let mut ops = Vec::new();
    let (mut i, mut j) = (n, m);

    while i > 0 || j > 0 {
        if i > 0 && j > 0 && a[i - 1] == b[j - 1] && dp[i][j] == dp[i - 1][j - 1] {
            ops.push(Edit::Match(a[i - 1]));
            i -= 1;
            j -= 1;
        } else if i > 0 && j > 0 && dp[i][j] == dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1 {
            ops.push(Edit::Replace(a[i - 1], b[j - 1]));
            i -= 1;
            j -= 1;
        } else if i > 0 && dp[i][j] == dp[i - 1][j] + 1 {
            ops.push(Edit::Delete(a[i - 1]));
            i -= 1;
        } else {
            ops.push(Edit::Insert(b[j - 1]));
            j -= 1;
        }
    }

    ops.reverse();
    (dp[n][m], ops)
}
```

This is standard Levenshtein edit distance with a left-to-right edit script. It operates on Unicode scalar values via `chars()`, not grapheme clusters.

Two ways it could fail under load:

1. **Memory exhaustion from the full DP matrix.**  
   It allocates `(n + 1) * (m + 1)` `usize` entries. For two 10k-character inputs, that is about 100M entries, roughly 800 MB per call, plus `Vec<Vec<_>>` overhead. Under concurrent requests this can quickly cause OOM or heavy swapping.

2. **CPU exhaustion from quadratic time complexity.**  
   The algorithm is `O(n * m)`. A few large inputs can saturate all CPU cores, causing latency spikes, timeouts, or backpressure collapse. If called on an async runtime worker thread, it can also block the executor because it never yields.

