### Alternative approach: Random-priority reservoir (heap-based)

Instead of deciding whether to replace a random slot, assign each stream item a random key and keep the `k` items with the smallest keys.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <stdint.h>

#define K 5

typedef struct {
    int item;
    uint64_t key;
} Node;

/* Max-heap ordered by key, so we can quickly remove the largest key. */
static Node heap[K];
static size_t heap_size = 0;

static uint64_t random64(void) {
    return ((uint64_t)rand() << 32) ^ (uint64_t)rand();
}

static void heap_swap(size_t a, size_t b) {
    Node t = heap[a];
    heap[a] = heap[b];
    heap[b] = t;
}

static void heap_up(size_t i) {
    while (i > 0) {
        size_t p = (i - 1) / 2;
        if (heap[p].key >= heap[i].key) break;
        heap_swap(p, i);
        i = p;
    }
}

static void heap_down(size_t i) {
    for (;;) {
        size_t l = 2 * i + 1;
        size_t r = 2 * i + 2;
        size_t largest = i;

        if (l < heap_size && heap[l].key > heap[largest].key)
            largest = l;
        if (r < heap_size && heap[r].key > heap[largest].key)
            largest = r;

        if (largest == i) break;
        heap_swap(i, largest);
        i = largest;
    }
}

static void add_item(int x) {
    Node n = { x, random64() };

    if (heap_size < K) {
        heap[heap_size] = n;
        heap_up(heap_size);
        heap_size++;
        return;
    }

    /* Keep only the k smallest keys. */
    if (n.key < heap[0].key) {
        heap[0] = n;
        heap_down(0);
    }
}

int main(void) {
    srand((unsigned)time(NULL));

    int x;
    while (scanf("%d", &x) == 1)
        add_item(x);

    for (size_t i = 0; i < heap_size; i++)
        printf("%d\n", heap[i].item);

    return 0;
}
```

### How it works

Each item gets an independent uniform random key. If you sort the entire stream by key and take the first `k`, every subset of size `k` is equally likely. The heap maintains exactly that first `k` set online without storing the whole stream.

### Complexity

| Method | Time | Space | Notes |
|---|---:|---:|---|
| **Algorithm R** (previous) | `O(n)` | `O(k)` | One random index per item after the first `k`; constant-time per item. |
| **Random-priority heap** | `O(n log k)` | `O(k)` | Each item may cause one heap operation, `O(log k)`. |

### Comparison

| Aspect | Algorithm R | Random-priority heap |
|---|---|---|
| Per-item time | `O(1)` | `O(1)` compare, `O(log k)` if replacement |
| Total time | `O(n)` | `O(n log k)` |
| Space | `O(k)` | `O(k)` |
| Stream length known? | No | No |
| Uniform sample? | Yes | Yes |
| Implementation | Very simple | Needs a heap |
| Weighted extension | Requires A-Res / A-Chao etc. | Easier: use key like `u^(1/w)` or `-ln(u)/w` |
| Best for | Standard unweighted streaming sample | Weighted sampling, mergeable/parallel samples, or priority-ordered samples |

### Conclusion

For plain uniform sampling from a stream of unknown length, **Algorithm R is usually preferred** because it runs in `O(n)` time with very low constant overhead and uses only `O(k)` space.

The **random-priority heap** is a useful alternative when you need more flexibility, such as weighted sampling, parallel/mergeable reservoirs, or maintaining an ordered random sample. It costs an extra `O(log k)` factor per item.

# Topological Sort in Kotlin

A topological sort orders the vertices of a directed acyclic graph (DAG) such that for every directed edge `u -> v`, `u` comes before `v`. If the graph contains a cycle, no valid ordering exists — we detect this by checking if the produced ordering contains all vertices.

Kotlin's `ArrayDeque` is used as a stack for the iterative DFS, avoiding recursion depth issues on large graphs.

```kotlin
package topsort

/**
 * Result of a topological sort.
 *
 * [order] is a valid topological ordering if [isAcyclic] is true.
 * Otherwise [order] contains the vertices that *could* be ordered
 * before the cycle was detected — it is not a full ordering.
 */
data class TopoResult(
    val isAcyclic: Boolean,
    val order: List<Int>
)

/**
 * Topologically sorts a directed graph given as an adjacency list.
 *
 * @param graph adjacency list: graph[u] is the list of vertices v with an edge u -> v.
 *              Vertices are assumed to be numbered 0 until graph.size.
 * @return a [TopoResult]. If the graph is acyclic, [TopoResult.isAcyclic]
 *         is true and [TopoResult.order] contains every vertex exactly once.
 *         If the graph has a cycle, [TopoResult.isAcyclic] is false.
 */
fun topologicalSort(graph: List<List<Int>>): TopoResult {
    val n = graph.size
    val visited = BooleanArray(n)      // fully processed
    val onStack = BooleanArray(n)      // currently in the DFS recursion stack
    val order = ArrayList<Int>(n)

    // Iterative DFS using an explicit stack of (vertex, childIndex) frames.
    for (start in 0 until n) {
        if (visited[start] || onStack[start]) continue

        val stack = ArrayDeque<Frame>()
        stack.addLast(Frame(start, 0))
        onStack[start] = true

        var cycleDetected = false

        while (stack.isNotEmpty()) {
            val top = stack.last()

            if (top.childIndex < graph[top.vertex].size) {
                val next = graph[top.vertex][top.childIndex]
                top.childIndex++

                require(next in 0 until n) {
                    "Edge from ${top.vertex} points to invalid vertex $next"
                }

                when {
                    onStack[next] -> {
                        // Back edge — we found a cycle.
                        cycleDetected = true
                    }
                    !visited[next] -> {
                        stack.addLast(Frame(next, 0))
                        onStack[next] = true
                    }
                }
            } else {
                // All children processed: emit this vertex.
                visited[top.vertex] = true
                onStack[top.vertex] = false
                order.add(top.vertex)
                stack.removeLast()
            }

            if (cycleDetected) break
        }

        if (cycleDetected) {
            // No valid ordering exists; return what we've accumulated so far.
            return TopoResult(isAcyclic = false, order = order)
        }
    }

    // DFS post-order produces reverse topological order.
    order.reverse()
    return TopoResult(isAcyclic = true, order = order)
}

private class Frame(val vertex: Int, var childIndex: Int)
```

## Notes on the algorithm

- **DFS post-order reversal**: Vertices are emitted when all their descendants are processed. Reversing the post-order list gives a valid topological order.
- **Cycle detection**: A directed graph has a cycle iff a back edge is encountered during DFS — that is, an edge pointing to a vertex still `onStack`.
- **Iterative**: We avoid recursion to sidestep `StackOverflowError` on deep graphs (e.g., a chain of 1M vertices).
- **Short-circuit**: When a cycle is found we bail out immediately.

## Unit tests

```kotlin
package topsort

import kotlin.test.Test
import kotlin.test.assertEquals
import kotlin.test.assertFalse
import kotlin.test.assertTrue

class TopologicalSortTest {

    /** Helper: verify that `order` respects every edge in `graph`. */
    private fun assertIsValidTopoOrder(graph: List<List<Int>>, order: List<Int>) {
        assertEquals(graph.size, order.size, "order must contain every vertex")
        assertEquals(graph.size, order.toSet().size, "order must not contain duplicates")
        val position = IntArray(graph.size)
        order.forEachIndexed { idx, v -> position[v] = idx }
        for (u in graph.indices) {
            for (v in graph[u]) {
                assertTrue(
                    position[u] < position[v],
                    "edge $u -> $v violated by order $order"
                )
            }
        }
    }

    // ---------- Happy path ----------

    @Test
    fun `empty graph is trivially sorted`() {
        val result = topologicalSort(emptyList())
        assertTrue(result.isAcyclic)
        assertEquals(emptyList(), result.order)
    }

    @Test
    fun `single vertex with no edges`() {
        val result = topologicalSort(listOf(emptyList()))
        assertTrue(result.isAcyclic)
        assertEquals(listOf(0), result.order)
    }

    @Test
    fun `isolated vertices are all included`() {
        val graph = listOf(emptyList<Int>(), emptyList(), emptyList())
        val result = topologicalSort(graph)
        assertTrue(result.isAcyclic)
        assertIsValidTopoOrder(graph, result.order)
    }

    @Test
    fun `simple diamond`() {
        // 0 -> 1, 0 -> 2, 1 -> 3, 2 -> 3
        val graph = listOf(
            listOf(1, 2),
            listOf(3),
            listOf(3),
            emptyList()
        )
        val result = topologicalSort(graph)
        assertTrue(result.isAcyclic)
        assertIsValidTopoOrder(graph, result.order)
        assertEquals(0, result.order.first(), "source should come first")
        assertEquals(3, result.order.last(), "sink should come last")
    }

    @Test
    fun `linear chain`() {
        val graph = listOf(listOf(1), listOf(2), listOf(3), emptyList())
        val result = topologicalSort(graph)
        assertTrue(result.isAcyclic)
        assertEquals(listOf(0, 1, 2, 3), result.order)
    }

    @Test
    fun `disconnected components`() {
        // Component A: 0 -> 1;  Component B: 2 -> 3
        val graph = listOf(
            listOf(1),
            emptyList(),
            listOf(3),
            emptyList()
        )
        val result = topologicalSort(graph)
        assertTrue(result.isAcyclic)
        assertIsValidTopoOrder(graph, result.order)
    }

    @Test
    fun `reverse-numbered chain`() {
        // Ensure reversal works: DFS visits 0->1->2->3 and emits 3,2,1,0.
        val graph = listOf(
            listOf(1),
            listOf(2),
            listOf(3),
            emptyList()
        )
        val result = topologicalSort(graph)
        assertTrue(result.isAcyclic)
        assertEquals(listOf(0, 1, 2, 3), result.order)
    }

    @Test
    fun `deep chain does not overflow the stack`() {
        val n = 100_000
        val graph = List(n) { i -> if (i < n - 1) listOf(i + 1) else emptyList() }
        val result = topologicalSort(graph)
        assertTrue(result.isAcyclic)
        assertEquals(n, result.order.size)
        assertEquals(0, result.order.first())
        assertEquals(n - 1, result.order.last())
    }

    @Test
    fun `dense DAG produces a valid ordering`() {
        val n = 200
        // Edge u -> v for every u < v, i.e. a total order by index.
        val graph = List(n) { u -> (u + 1 until n).toList() }
        val result = topologicalSort(graph)
        assertTrue(result.isAcyclic)
        assertEquals((0 until n).toList(), result.order)
    }

    // ---------- Cycles ----------

    @Test
    fun `self loop is a cycle`() {
        val result = topologicalSort(listOf(listOf(0)))
        assertFalse(result.isAcyclic)
    }

    @Test
    fun `two-node cycle`() {
        val result = topologicalSort(listOf(listOf(1), listOf(0)))
        assertFalse(result.isAcyclic)
    }

    @Test
    fun `three-node cycle`() {
        val result = topologicalSort(listOf(listOf(1), listOf(2), listOf(0)))
        assertFalse(result.isAcyclic)
    }

    @Test
    fun `cycle buried inside a larger graph`() {
        // 0 -> 1 -> 2 -> 1 (cycle between 1 and 2)
        val graph = listOf(
            listOf(1),
            listOf(2),
            listOf(1)
        )
        val result = topologicalSort(graph)
        assertFalse(result.isAcyclic)
    }

    @Test
    fun `cycle in a graph where some vertices are acyclic`() {
        // Vertex 3 is acyclic leaf; 0<->1 is a cycle.
        val graph = listOf(
            listOf(1),
            listOf(0),
            listOf(3),
            emptyList()
        )
        val result = topologicalSort(graph)
        assertFalse(result.isAcyclic)
    }

    // ---------- Edge cases / invalid input ----------

    @Test
    fun `edge pointing to an invalid vertex throws`() {
        val graph = listOf(listOf(5))  // vertex 5 does not exist
        var threw = false
        try {
            topologicalSort(graph)
        } catch (e: IllegalArgumentException) {
            threw = true
        }
        assertTrue(threw, "expected IllegalArgumentException for invalid edge")
    }

    @Test
    fun `graph with no edges and many vertices`() {
        val n = 1000
        val graph = List(n) { emptyList<Int>() }
        val result = topologicalSort(graph)
        assertTrue(result.isAcyclic)
        assertEquals(n, result.order.size)
    }
}
```

## Testing strategy

The suite covers:

1. **Trivial cases** — empty graph, single vertex, isolated vertices.
2. **Classic DAGs** — chains, diamonds, disconnected components, reverse-ordered chains (to exercise reversal).
3. **Correctness invariant** — `assertIsValidTopoOrder` checks that every edge is respected and every vertex appears exactly once, rather than asserting a specific (implementation-defined) ordering.
4. **Cycle detection** — self-loops, 2- and 3-node cycles, cycles buried in otherwise-acyclic graphs, and cycles alongside valid acyclic regions.
5. **Input validation** — edges pointing to non-existent vertices.
6. **Stress / robustness** — a 100k-node chain guards against stack overflow (this is where the iterative approach matters), and a dense 200-vertex DAG checks behavior on many edges.

Here’s a PostgreSQL implementation. It parses ISO 8601 durations such as `P3DT4H5M` into seconds. It deliberately rejects years and months because they are not fixed-length and cannot be converted to seconds unambiguously.

```sql
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION iso8601_duration_to_seconds(duration text)
RETURNS numeric
LANGUAGE plpgsql
IMMUTABLE
STRICT
AS $$
DECLARE
    sign numeric := 1;
    s text;
    date_part text;
    matches text[];
    weeks numeric := 0;
    days numeric := 0;
    hours numeric := 0;
    minutes numeric := 0;
    seconds numeric := 0;
BEGIN
    s := upper(trim(duration));

    IF s = '' THEN
        RAISE EXCEPTION 'Duration must not be empty';
    END IF;

    -- Optional leading sign
    IF left(s, 1) = '-' THEN
        sign := -1;
        s := substr(s, 2);
    ELSIF left(s, 1) = '+' THEN
        s := substr(s, 2);
    END IF;

    -- Must start with P
    IF left(s, 1) <> 'P' THEN
        RAISE EXCEPTION 'Invalid ISO 8601 duration: %', duration;
    END IF;

    s := substr(s, 2); -- remove P

    IF s = '' THEN
        RAISE EXCEPTION 'Invalid ISO 8601 duration: %', duration;
    END IF;

    -- Weeks: PnW is a special ISO 8601 form and cannot be combined with other components
    IF s ~ '^[0-9]+(\.[0-9]+)?W$' THEN
        weeks := (left(s, length(s) - 1))::numeric;
        RETURN sign * weeks * 7 * 24 * 60 * 60;
    END IF;

    -- Reject multiple T separators
    IF s ~ 'T.*T' THEN
        RAISE EXCEPTION 'Invalid ISO 8601 duration: multiple T separators: %', duration;
    END IF;

    -- Years and months are ambiguous for seconds conversion.
    -- Check only the date part before T, so PT1M (1 minute) is still allowed.
    date_part := split_part(s, 'T', 1);
    IF date_part ~ '[YM]' THEN
        RAISE EXCEPTION 'Years and months are not supported because they are not fixed-length: %', duration;
    END IF;

    -- Reject T with no following time component, e.g. P3DT or PT
    IF s LIKE '%T' THEN
        RAISE EXCEPTION 'Invalid ISO 8601 duration: T must be followed by a time component: %', duration;
    END IF;

    -- Parse optional D, then optional T with optional H, M, S
    matches := regexp_match(
        s,
        '^(?:(\d+(?:\.\d+)?)D)?(?:T(?:(\d+(?:\.\d+)?)H)?(?:(\d+(?:\.\d+)?)M)?(?:(\d+(?:\.\d+)?)S)?)?$'
    );

    IF matches IS NULL THEN
        RAISE EXCEPTION 'Invalid ISO 8601 duration: %', duration;
    END IF;

    -- Ensure at least one component is present
    IF matches[1] IS NULL
       AND matches[2] IS NULL
       AND matches[3] IS NULL
       AND matches[4] IS NULL THEN
        RAISE EXCEPTION 'Invalid ISO 8601 duration: no components: %', duration;
    END IF;

    days    := COALESCE(matches[1]::numeric, 0);
    hours   := COALESCE(matches[2]::numeric, 0);
    minutes := COALESCE(matches[3]::numeric, 0);
    seconds := COALESCE(matches[4]::numeric, 0);

    RETURN sign * (
        days    * 24 * 60 * 60 +
        hours   * 60 * 60 +
        minutes * 60 +
        seconds
    );
END;
$$;
```

Example usage:

```sql
SELECT iso8601_duration_to_seconds('P3DT4H5M');  -- 273900
SELECT iso8601_duration_to_seconds('PT1H30M');   -- 5400
SELECT iso8601_duration_to_seconds('P1W');       -- 604800
SELECT iso8601_duration_to_seconds('PT0.5S');    -- 0.5
```

