# Edit Distance with Edit Script

Below is a complete C++ implementation of the **Levenshtein edit distance** with full **backtracking to recover the edit script** (the sequence of operations that transforms one string into another).

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

// A single edit operation in the script.
enum class Op { Match, Substitute, Insert, Delete };

struct Edit {
    Op op;
    char from; // character in source (for Match/Sub/Delete)
    char to;   // character in target (for Match/Sub/Insert)
};

// Compute edit distance and produce the edit script.
// The script transforms `a` into `b`.
std::pair<int, std::vector<Edit>> edit_distance(const std::string& a,
                                                const std::string& b) {
    const int n = static_cast<int>(a.size());
    const int m = static_cast<int>(b.size());

    // dp[i][j] = edit distance between a[0..i) and b[0..j)
    std::vector<std::vector<int>> dp(n + 1, std::vector<int>(m + 1));

    for (int i = 0; i <= n; ++i) dp[i][0] = i;
    for (int j = 0; j <= m; ++j) dp[0][j] = j;

    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {
        for (int j = 1; j <= m; ++j) {
            if (a[i - 1] == b[j - 1]) {
                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1];
            } else {
                dp[i][j] = 1 + std::min({dp[i - 1][j - 1], // substitute
                                         dp[i - 1][j],     // delete
                                         dp[i][j - 1]});   // insert
            }
        }
    }

    // Backtrack from (n, m) to (0, 0) to recover the operations.
    std::vector<Edit> script;
    int i = n, j = m;
    while (i > 0 || j > 0) {
        if (i > 0 && j > 0 && a[i - 1] == b[j - 1] &&
            dp[i][j] == dp[i - 1][j - 1]) {
            script.push_back({Op::Match, a[i - 1], b[j - 1]});
            --i; --j;
        } else if (i > 0 && j > 0 && dp[i][j] == dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1) {
            script.push_back({Op::Substitute, a[i - 1], b[j - 1]});
            --i; --j;
        } else if (i > 0 && dp[i][j] == dp[i - 1][j] + 1) {
            script.push_back({Op::Delete, a[i - 1], '\0'});
            --i;
        } else { // j > 0
            script.push_back({Op::Insert, '\0', b[j - 1]});
            --j;
        }
    }

    std::reverse(script.begin(), script.end());
    return {dp[n][m], script};
}

// ---- Usage example ----
int main() {
    std::string a = "kitten";
    std::string b = "sitting";

    auto [dist, script] = edit_distance(a, b);

    std::cout << "Edit distance between \"" << a << "\" and \"" << b
              << "\" is " << dist << "\n\nEdit script:\n";

    for (const auto& e : script) {
        switch (e.op) {
            case Op::Match:
                std::cout << "  match      '" << e.from << "'\n";
                break;
            case Op::Substitute:
                std::cout << "  substitute '" << e.from << "' -> '" << e.to << "'\n";
                break;
            case Op::Insert:
                std::cout << "  insert     +'" << e.to << "'\n";
                break;
            case Op::Delete:
                std::cout << "  delete     -'" << e.from << "'\n";
                break;
        }
    }
    return 0;
}
```

### Expected output

```
Edit distance between "kitten" and "sitting" is 3

Edit script:
  substitute 'k' -> 's'
  match      'i'
  match      't'
  match      't'
  substitute 'e' -> 'i'
  insert     +'n'
  match      'g'
```

### Notes

- **Complexity:** O(n·m) time and space. For very long strings, use the two-row Hirschberg variant or a banded DP.
- **Tie-breaking:** When multiple neighbour cells tie (e.g. equal cost for substitution vs. delete+insert), the test order decides which operation is chosen. The code above prefers match → substitute → delete → insert, which yields compact, natural scripts. Swap the `if` clauses if you prefer a different preference (e.g. favoring insert over delete).
- **Script direction:** The returned script transforms `a` into `b`. Reverse the parameters if you need the opposite direction, or invert each op (`Insert`↔`Delete`, `Substitute`/`Match` unchanged).
- **Match entries** are included in the script so it fully describes the alignment. Drop them in the loop if you only want the actual edits.

Here’s a TypeScript retry wrapper with exponential backoff, capped delay, configurable jitter, abort support, and test-friendly dependency injection for `random` and timers.

