# Minimal Bloom Filter in C++

```cpp
#include <cstdint>
#include <cmath>
#include <vector>
#include <string>
#include <functional>

class BloomFilter {
public:
    // n = expected items, p = desired false-positive rate
    BloomFilter(size_t n, double p) {
        // m = -(n * ln p) / (ln 2)^2   (bits)
        m_ = static_cast<size_t>(-(static_cast<double>(n) * std::log(p)) /
                                 (std::log(2.0) * std::log(2.0)));
        if (m_ < 1) m_ = 1;

        // k = (m / n) * ln 2   (hash functions)
        k_ = static_cast<size_t>(std::round((static_cast<double>(m_) / n) * std::log(2.0)));
        if (k_ < 1) k_ = 1;

        bits_.assign((m_ + 63) / 64, 0ULL);
    }

    void insert(const std::string& key) {
        for (size_t i = 0; i < k_; ++i) {
            size_t idx = hash(key, i) % m_;
            bits_[idx >> 6] |= (1ULL << (idx & 63));
        }
    }

    bool contains(const std::string& key) const {
        for (size_t i = 0; i < k_; ++i) {
            size_t idx = hash(key, i) % m_;
            if (!(bits_[idx >> 6] & (1ULL << (idx & 63)))) return false;
        }
        return true;
    }

private:
    // Double hashing: h_i(k) = h1(k) + i * h2(k)
    size_t hash(const std::string& key, size_t i) const {
        std::hash<std::string> h;
        size_t h1 = h(key);
        size_t h2 = h(key + "#salt") | 1; // ensure odd so it's coprime-ish
        return h1 + i * h2;
    }

    size_t m_;                  // number of bits
    size_t k_;                  // number of hash functions
    std::vector<uint64_t> bits_;
};
```

**Usage:**
```cpp
BloomFilter bf(1'000'000, 0.01); // 1M items, 1% FP rate
bf.insert("hello");
assert(bf.contains("hello"));    // true
```

---

## Two ways it fails under load

### 1. Saturating the bit array (over-insertion)
The math assumes you insert at most `n` items. If the system admits more — say 10× the expected load because growth wasn't bounded — the bit array fills up. The false-positive rate is roughly `(1 - e^(-kn/m))^k`; as actual `n` exceeds `m/k`, this approaches 1.0. Practically: a "negative" cache lookup that used to correctly reject 99% of misses now rejects almost none, so the backend gets hammered with the same traffic the bloom filter was supposed to absorb. Classic cascading-failure pattern.

### 2. Hash-collision pressure from hot keys / adversarial input
`std::hash<std::string>` is deterministic and not seeded per-process. Under load (or with attacker-controlled keys), collisions in `h1` and `h2` cluster indices together. Even before the array is full, a small set of colliding keys can flip the same bits and raise the *effective* FP rate far above the designed `p`. Worse, since the filter is read-only under concurrency in this minimal version, a burst of inserts from one writer thread while others read will also **race on `bits_`** — torn reads of a `uint64_t` word can return a partially-set mask, giving false negatives (the one thing a bloom filter is *not* supposed to do). Fix: either shard by lock, or make the bit array atomic — but be aware atomicity alone doesn't fix the saturation problem.

```ts
export function parseCSV(input: string): string[][] {
  const rows: string[][] = [];
  let row: string[] = [];
  let field = "";
  let inQuotes = false;
  let fieldTouched = false;

  for (let i = 0; i < input.length; i++) {
    const ch = input[i];

    if (inQuotes) {
      if (ch === '"') {
        if (input[i + 1] === '"') {
          field += '"';
          i++;
        } else {
          inQuotes = false;
        }
      } else {
        field += ch;
      }
      fieldTouched = true;
      continue;
    }

    if (ch === '"') {
      inQuotes = true;
      fieldTouched = true;
    } else if (ch === ",") {
      row.push(field);
      field = "";
      fieldTouched = false;
    } else if (ch === "\n") {
      row.push(field);
      rows.push(row);
      row = [];
      field = "";
      fieldTouched = false;
    } else if (ch === "\r") {
      if (input[i + 1] !== "\n") {
        row.push(field);
        rows.push(row);
        row = [];
        field = "";
        fieldTouched = false;
      }
    } else {
      field += ch;
      fieldTouched = true;
    }
  }

  if (fieldTouched || row.length > 0) {
    row.push(field);
    rows.push(row);
  }

  return rows;
}
```

Example:

```ts
parseCSV('a,"b,c","d""e"\n1,2,3');
// [
//   ['a', 'b,c', 'd"e'],
//   ['1', '2', '3']
// ]
```

Two ways it could fail under load:

1. **Memory blowup / OOM**: It buffers the entire input and returns the entire parsed `string[][]`. A multi-GB CSV, or many concurrent large requests, will keep the raw string, arrays, and field strings in memory at once. It also has no streaming/backpressure.

2. **Event-loop blocking / CPU starvation**: Parsing is fully synchronous. A very large CSV blocks Node’s single thread, causing timeouts and latency for all other requests. Large fields also cause heavy per-character string concatenation/GC pressure, which can make throughput collapse under load.

