```java
import java.util.concurrent.locks.Condition;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

public class BoundedBlockingQueue<E> {
    private final E[] buffer;
    private final int capacity;

    private int head;   // next index to read
    private int tail;   // next index to write
    private int size;   // number of elements currently stored

    private final ReentrantLock lock = new ReentrantLock();
    private final Condition notEmpty = lock.newCondition();
    private final Condition notFull = lock.newCondition();

    @SuppressWarnings("unchecked")
    public BoundedBlockingQueue(int capacity) {
        if (capacity <= 0) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("capacity must be > 0");
        }
        this.capacity = capacity;
        this.buffer = (E[]) new Object[capacity];
    }

    public void put(E item) throws InterruptedException {
        if (item == null) {
            throw new NullPointerException("null items are not supported");
        }

        lock.lockInterruptibly();
        try {
            while (size == capacity) {
                notFull.await(); // wait until there is space
            }

            buffer[tail] = item;
            tail = (tail + 1) % capacity;
            size++;

            notEmpty.signal(); // wake one waiting consumer
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }

    public E get() throws InterruptedException {
        lock.lockInterruptibly();
        try {
            while (size == 0) {
                notEmpty.await(); // wait until there is an item
            }

            E item = buffer[head];
            buffer[head] = null; // allow GC
            head = (head + 1) % capacity;
            size--;

            notFull.signal(); // wake one waiting producer
            return item;
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }

    public int size() {
        lock.lock();
        try {
            return size;
        } finally {
            lock.unlock();
        }
    }
}
```

### How it works

- A circular array `buffer` stores up to `capacity` elements.
- `head` points to the next element to remove.
- `tail` points to the next slot to insert into.
- `size` tracks the current number of elements.
- A single `ReentrantLock` protects all mutable state, so the queue is thread-safe.
- Two `Condition`s are used:
  - `notEmpty`: consumers wait here when the queue is empty.
  - `notFull`: producers wait here when the queue is full.

Both `put` and `get` use `while` loops around `await()`, which is required because threads can wake up spuriously and because another thread may consume/produce the resource before the awakened thread reacquires the lock.

### Time complexity

- `put(E)`: `O(1)` once the thread can proceed. It may block indefinitely while the queue is full.
- `get()`: `O(1)` once the thread can proceed. It may block indefinitely while the queue is empty.
- Lock acquisition and signaling are constant-time operations in principle, though actual contention can affect latency.

### Space complexity

- `O(capacity)` for the backing array.
- `O(1)` additional bookkeeping fields: `head`, `tail`, `size`, lock, and conditions.
- Waiting threads consume thread/stack resources, but that is not part of the queue’s element storage complexity.

In production Java code, `ArrayBlockingQueue<E>` implements essentially this behavior.

