Below is a minimal Rayon-based inclusive parallel prefix sum over a mutable `u64` slice.

```rust
use rayon::prelude::*;

pub fn par_prefix_sum(xs: &mut [u64]) {
    if xs.len() <= 1 {
        return;
    }

    let mid = xs.len() / 2;
    let (left, right) = xs.split_at_mut(mid);

    // Prefix-sum each half independently.
    rayon::join(|| par_prefix_sum(left), || par_prefix_sum(right));

    // Add the total of the left half to every element of the right half.
    let left_sum = *left.last().unwrap();
    right.par_iter_mut().for_each(|x| *x += left_sum);
}
```

This computes an inclusive scan: after the call, `xs[i]` is the sum of the original `xs[0..=i]`.

### Two ways it could fail under load

1. **Task explosion / scheduler overhead**  
   The recursion creates a Rayon task for every split down to length 1, so for an array of length `n` it creates O(n) tasks. Under heavy load, this floods the thread pool with tiny tasks, causing excessive scheduling overhead, memory pressure, and poor load balancing. A practical version needs a sequential cutoff, e.g. `if xs.len() < 4096 { sequential_scan(xs); return; }`.

2. **False sharing / memory-bandwidth contention**  
   The final `right.par_iter_mut().for_each(|x| *x += left_sum)` writes adjacent elements in parallel. Different threads may update values on the same cache line, causing cache-line ping-pong and severe slowdown under high concurrency. At scale, the parallel writes can also saturate memory bandwidth, making the scan slower than a sequential version.

