rand = "0.8"
# For reproducible seeding in tests; drop in production if you use ThreadRng.
rand_chacha = "0.3"
```

```rust
use rand::thread_rng;

let mut sampler = ReservoirSampler::new(100, thread_rng());
for record in stream {
    sampler.offer(record);
}
let sample: Vec<_> = sampler.into_sample();
```

## Design decisions worth calling out

**Why `Vec<T>` and not `[Option<T>; k]`?** The `Vec` lets `len()` distinguish "still filling" from "full" for free, and the fill phase pushes into preallocated capacity so there's no reallocation churn. If you need a fixed-size array at the ABI level (e.g. FFI), wrap this behind your own adapter.

**Why own the RNG?** Threading `&mut R` through every `offer` is noisy at call sites. Owning it also makes the type trivially `Send` when `R: Send`, which matters if you're sampling per-worker in a thread pool. If you need to share RNG state across samplers, take `R: Rng` as a `&mut R` instead — the trade-off is ergonomic, pick whichever your call sites prefer.

**Why `u64` for `seen` and not `u128` or `usize`?** `usize` is 32-bit on some targets and would overflow on streams that run for weeks. `u64` is enough for any realistic stream (see the comment above). `random_range(0..seen)` internally handles the widening correctly, so we don't need to do the `u128` dance ourselves.

**Algorithm R vs. Algorithm L.** Algorithm R is `O(1)` amortized with one RNG call per item. Algorithm L (skip-counting) does better on huge streams where RNG calls dominate — it computes how many items to skip before the next replacement, giving `O(k · (1 + log(n/k)))` total RNG calls. If your `offer` is on a hot path consuming tens of millions of items/sec and RNG is your bottleneck, switch to Algorithm L. For most production services, Algorithm R is fine and much easier to review.

**Uniformity guarantee.** After `n` items, item at position `i` is in the reservoir with probability exactly `k / n`. This holds even though we don't know `n` in advance — that's the magic of the algorithm, and it's why the replacement probability must be `k / seen` at step `seen`, not `k / n` (which we don't have).

