#include <cuda_runtime.h>

// Per-thread debouncer: returns true at most once per window_cycles.
// window_cycles is in SM clock cycles (clock64()).
struct Debouncer {
    unsigned long long window_cycles;
    unsigned long long last = 0;

    __device__ bool fire() {
        unsigned long long now = clock64();
        if (now - last < window_cycles) return false;
        last = now;
        return true;
    }
};

// Usage inside a kernel:
__global__ void kernel() {
    Debouncer d{1000000}; // ~1e6 cycles window
    for (int i = 0; i < N; ++i) {
        if (d.fire()) {
            // expensive/coalesced work
        }
    }
}
```

This coalesces repeated calls from the same thread within the cycle window. If you need cross-thread or cross-block coalescing, you must move `last` into shared/global memory and synchronize with atomics.

Two ways it can fail under load:

1. **`clock64()` is per-SM, not a global wall clock.**  
   Under load, blocks run on different SMs with independent, unsynchronized counters. Threads on different SMs can see completely different “times,” so the same wall-clock window is not consistent across the GPU. Calls that should be coalesced may all fire, or none may fire.

2. **Scaling to shared/global state with atomics causes contention and races.**  
   If you make the debouncer global by using atomics on a shared `last` timestamp, a high call rate serializes on that atomic. Without careful fencing/ordering, multiple threads can read the old timestamp and all decide to fire, or updates can be lost, defeating the debounce.

