### Alternative approach: one thread per cell with shared-memory bitmasks

Instead of assigning 27 threads to the 27 Sudoku units (9 rows, 9 columns, 9 boxes), use **81 threads**, one per cell. Each thread reads its cell and atomically sets a bit in three shared bitmasks: one for its row, one for its column, and one for its 3×3 box. If the bit was already set, a duplicate exists.

```cpp
__global__ void validateSudokuCellKernel(const int* __restrict__ board,
                                         int* __restrict__ valid)
{
    __shared__ unsigned int rowMask[9];
    __shared__ unsigned int colMask[9];
    __shared__ unsigned int boxMask[9];

    const int tid = threadIdx.x;

    // Initialize shared bitmasks
    if (tid < 27) {
        if (tid < 9)        rowMask[tid]      = 0;
        else if (tid < 18)  colMask[tid - 9]  = 0;
        else                boxMask[tid - 18] = 0;
    }
    __syncthreads();

    if (tid >= 81) return;

    const int r = tid / 9;
    const int c = tid % 9;
    const int v = board[tid];

    if (v == 0) return;                   // empty cell
    if (v < 1 || v > 9) {                 // malformed input
        atomicExch(valid, 0);
        return;
    }

    const unsigned int bit = 1u << (v - 1);
    const int b = (r / 3) * 3 + (c / 3);  // box index 0..8

    if (atomicOr(&rowMask[r], bit) & bit) atomicExch(valid, 0);
    if (atomicOr(&colMask[c], bit) & bit) atomicExch(valid, 0);
    if (atomicOr(&boxMask[b], bit) & bit) atomicExch(valid, 0);
}
```

Launch with one block of, say, 128 threads (81 active threads process cells, first 27 initialize shared memory).

---

### Comparison

| Aspect | Original (27 threads, one per unit) | Alternative (81 threads, one per cell) |
|---|---|---|
| **Parallelism** | 27 threads | 81 threads |
| **Work per thread** | Scans up to 9 cells sequentially | Processes exactly 1 cell |
| **Shared memory** | None | 3 × 9 = 27 `unsigned int` |
| **Synchronization** | None | One `__syncthreads()` after initialization |
| **Atomic operations** | Only on invalid board | Shared-memory `atomicOr` per non-empty cell (up to 3 per cell) |
| **Memory access** | Row/column/box strides, some uncoalesced | Fully coalesced: thread `tid` reads `board[tid]` |
| **Complexity (9×9)** | O(1) time, O(1) space | O(1) time, O(1) space |
| **Generalized n²×n²** | O(N) time, O(1) space | O(N) time, O(1) space |
| **Best for** | Single 9×9 board; minimal overhead | Larger boards or many boards in a batch; better memory coalescing and parallelism |

For a standard 9×9 Sudoku, the original approach is usually faster because its overhead is tiny: only 27 threads, no shared memory, no synchronization, and no shared atomics. The cell-based alternative exposes more parallelism and coalesces global reads, but the atomic operations on shared bitmasks and the `__syncthreads()` add overhead that can dominate on such a small problem. The alternative becomes attractive when validating many Sudoku boards in parallel (e.g., one block per board) or when generalizing to larger board sizes where higher parallelism helps.

