# Minimal Sudoku Validator

```java
public static boolean isValidSudoku(int[][] board) {
    boolean[][] rows = new boolean[9][9];
    boolean[][] cols = new boolean[9][9];
    boolean[][] boxes = new boolean[9][9];

    for (int r = 0; r < 9; r++) {
        for (int c = 0; c < 9; c++) {
            int v = board[r][c];
            if (v == 0) continue;          // 0 = empty cell
            if (v < 1 || v > 9) return false;

            int b = (r / 3) * 3 + (c / 3);
            int idx = v - 1;

            if (rows[r][idx] || cols[c][idx] || boxes[b][idx]) return false;
            rows[r][idx] = cols[c][idx] = boxes[b][idx] = true;
        }
    }
    return true;
}
```

**Why this shape:** O(81) time, O(1) space, no allocation per call (the three `boolean[9][9]` are the only allocation, and they could be hoisted into thread-locals for a real service). Values are 1–9; `0` means empty.

---

## Two ways it could fail under load

1. **Per-call array allocation → GC pressure.**
   Every invocation allocates three 81-byte arrays, plus the object headers. At, say, 50k requests/sec across threads, that's ~150k short-lived arrays/sec feeding the young generation. Throughput is fine on a warm JVM, but under sustained load the allocation rate starts driving frequent minor GCs, and if the request rate spikes the allocator/collector can dominate the profile. The fix is trivial — reuse preallocated buffers (ThreadLocal or a small pool) and `Arrays.fill(false)` between calls — but the *minimal* version as written doesn't do that.

2. **Unbounded input trust → one malformed board stalls a worker.**
   The function assumes `board` is 9×9, but nothing enforces it. A single caller passing a jagged or larger array either throws `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException` or, worse, walks rows/columns the caller controls — turning a "cheap, constant-time" validator into an unbounded loop. On a thread pool, one such request occupies a worker for an attacker-chosen duration; enough of them and every worker is stuck, producing classic head-of-line blocking. The fix is a length check at the top (or validation at the API boundary), which the minimal version omits.

A third, subtler one for free: the `board` reference is not defensively copied, so if callers mutate it concurrently (e.g., a shared cached board being updated while validators read it), you get torn reads against a grid that was never a valid Sudoku snapshot — a correctness bug that only shows up at load.

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

struct EditOperation {
    enum class Type {
        Match,
        Insert,
        Delete,
        Substitute
    };

    Type type;
    char from; // used for Match/Delete/Substitute
    char to;   // used for Match/Insert/Substitute
};

// Returns {edit_distance, edit_script}.
// The script transforms `source` into `target`.
std::pair<int, std::vector<EditOperation>>
editDistanceWithScript(const std::string& source, const std::string& target) {
    const int n = static_cast<int>(source.size());
    const int m = static_cast<int>(target.size());

    // dp[i][j] = edit distance between source[0..i) and target[0..j)
    std::vector<std::vector<int>> dp(n + 1, std::vector<int>(m + 1, 0));

    for (int i = 0; i <= n; ++i) {
        dp[i][0] = i; // delete all characters from source
    }
    for (int j = 0; j <= m; ++j) {
        dp[0][j] = j; // insert all characters from target
    }

    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {
        for (int j = 1; j <= m; ++j) {
            if (source[i - 1] == target[j - 1]) {
                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1];
            } else {
                dp[i][j] = 1 + std::min({
                    dp[i - 1][j],      // delete source[i - 1]
                    dp[i][j - 1],      // insert target[j - 1]
                    dp[i - 1][j - 1]   // substitute source[i - 1] -> target[j - 1]
                });
            }
        }
    }

    std::vector<EditOperation> script;

    // Backtrack from dp[n][m] to dp[0][0].
    int i = n;
    int j = m;

    while (i > 0 || j > 0) {
        if (i > 0 && j > 0 &&
            source[i - 1] == target[j - 1] &&
            dp[i][j] == dp[i - 1][j - 1]) {
            script.push_back({EditOperation::Type::Match,
                              source[i - 1], target[j - 1]});
            --i;
            --j;
        } else if (i > 0 && j > 0 &&
                   dp[i][j] == dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1) {
            script.push_back({EditOperation::Type::Substitute,
                              source[i - 1], target[j - 1]});
            --i;
            --j;
        } else if (i > 0 && dp[i][j] == dp[i - 1][j] + 1) {
            script.push_back({EditOperation::Type::Delete,
                              source[i - 1], '\0'});
            --i;
        } else if (j > 0 && dp[i][j] == dp[i][j - 1] + 1) {
            script.push_back({EditOperation::Type::Insert,
                              '\0', target[j - 1]});
            --j;
        } else {
            // Should not be reached if the DP table is correct.
            break;
        }
    }

    std::reverse(script.begin(), script.end());
    return {dp[n][m], script};
}

void printScript(const std::vector<EditOperation>& script) {
    for (const auto& op : script) {
        switch (op.type) {
            case EditOperation::Type::Match:
                std::cout << "Match      '" << op.from << "'\n";
                break;
            case EditOperation::Type::Insert:
                std::cout << "Insert     '" << op.to << "'\n";
                break;
            case EditOperation::Type::Delete:
                std::cout << "Delete     '" << op.from << "'\n";
                break;
            case EditOperation::Type::Substitute:
                std::cout << "Substitute '" << op.from
                          << "' -> '" << op.to << "'\n";
                break;
        }
    }
}

int main() {
    std::string source = "kitten";
    std::string target = "sitting";

    auto [distance, script] = editDistanceWithScript(source, target);

    std::cout << "Edit distance: " << distance << "\n\n";
    std::cout << "Edit script:\n";
    printScript(script);

    return 0;
}
```

Example output for `"kitten"` → `"sitting"`:

```text
Edit distance: 3

Edit script:
Substitute 'k' -> 's'
Match      'i'
Match      't'
Match      't'
Substitute 'e' -> 'i'
Insert     'n'
Match      'g'
```

### Complexity

Let `n = source.size()` and `m = target.size()`.

- **Time complexity:** `O(n * m)`
  - Filling the DP table takes `O(n * m)`.
  - Backtracking to build the script takes `O(n + m)`.
  - Overall: `O(n * m)`.

- **Space complexity:** `O(n * m)`
  - The full DP table has `(n + 1) * (m + 1)` integers.
  - The edit script uses `O(n + m)` extra space.
  - Overall: `O(n * m)`.

If only the edit distance is needed, space can be reduced to `O(min(n, m))` by keeping only two rows. However, reconstructing the full edit script normally requires storing the full DP table, unless a divide-and-conquer approach such as Hirschberg’s algorithm is used.

