Here's a second approach, followed by a comparison.

## Alternative: Hillis–Steele (parallel doubling)

Each round `k` doubles how far left each element looks, adding its value from `2^(k-1)` positions back. After `⌈log₂ n⌉` rounds every element holds its prefix sum.

```go
// InclusiveScanHillisSteele computes an inclusive prefix sum using the
// Hillis–Steele (parallel doubling) algorithm. It modifies data in place.
//
// Each round halves the number of unresolved dependencies. Round k = 0, 1,
// 2, ... does data[i] += data[i - 2^k] using values from the *previous*
// round, so a snapshot is required — an in-place version would race (some
// goroutines would read cells already updated this round). We double-buffer
// to avoid that.
//
// Work is O(n log n); depth is O(log n). Prefer this when n is large and
// many cores / wide SIMD lanes are available. For CPU-bound moderate-n
// workloads, the chunked two-pass scan usually wins on total work and
// memory traffic.
func InclusiveScanHillisSteele(data []int64) {
	n := len(data)
	if n < 2 {
