## Comparison

| Property | Chunked two-pass | Hillis–Steele |
|---|---|---|
| Total work | **O(n)** (≈ 2n adds) | O(n log n) |
| Depth / barriers | O(n/k) per pass + O(k) sequential | O(log n) barriers |
| Extra memory | O(k) ≈ O(GOMAXPROCS) | O(n) full buffer |
| Rollback on panics | easy (in place) | easier (original untouched until final copy) |
| Code complexity | higher (chunk arithmetic, two passes) | lower (one loop body) |
| Tuning knobs | `minParallelLen`, chunk count | `minParallelLen` only |
| Memory traffic | reads/writes data ~2× per element | ~log n passes over the whole array |
| Float associativity | sequential within chunk, tree across chunks | pairwise-tree-like, often lower error |

### How to choose

- **CPU-bound, memory-bandwidth-limited, `n` in the millions, cores ≲ 64**: chunked scan. It touches memory twice and does 2n adds. Hillis–Steele reads the full array every round — for `n = 10⁶`, ~20 rounds × 8 MB ≈ 160 MB of traffic versus ~16 MB. On a normal x86 server the chunked version usually wins by a wide margin past a few thousand elements.
- **Massively parallel lanes (GPU, AVX-512, many-core NUMA box with `n` huge)**: Hillis–Steele. The uniform per-element body maps cleanly to SIMD; you eat the extra work to get predictable `log n` depth with no sequential bottleneck. The tree-shaped addition also tends to have better numerical error for floats than a single linear accumulation.
- **Simplicity-first or small-to-medium `n`**: Hillis–Steele is easier to read and audit correctly, at the cost of an O(n) scratch buffer and the double-buffer swap logic.

### One gotcha shared by both

`minParallelLen` is doing double duty: it is both the “fall back to sequential” threshold and the parallelism floor. For the chunked scan, benchmark both `minParallelLen` **and** the chunk count `k` — the sequential pass over `chunkSums` is O(k), so picking `k` too large eats into the parallel phase. A rule of thumb that survives most hardware is `k = GOMAXPROCS`, which is what the earlier implementation caps at.

