```

## Edge cases covered

| Case | Why it matters |
|---|---|
| Single call runs exactly once | Baseline correctness |
| Multiple rapid calls → only last runs | Core debounce semantics |
| Calls separated by more than the window both run | Debounce shouldn't swallow distinct events |
| `cancel()` prevents execution | Essential for view teardown / disposal |
| Calling after `cancel()` still works | Cancel isn't sticky |
| `flush()` runs immediately | Needed when you want to force-fire (e.g. on `viewWillDisappear`) |
| `flush()` then delay doesn't double-run | Ensures the work item is truly consumed |
| Zero delay | Verifies coalescing still holds even when delay is ~instant |
| Re-entrant call from inside the action | Prevents lock-held-during-callback bugs |
| 100 concurrent calls | Locks actually protect shared state |

## Notes on the design

- **`NSLock` around `workItem`** — `call`, `cancel`, and `flush` all mutate shared state; without the lock, concurrent calls can drop work items or run stale ones.
- **`DispatchWorkItem`** instead of `Timer` — simpler cancellation semantics (no run-loop dependency), and it composes with `DispatchQueue`.
- **Injecting the queue** — critical for testing. Defaulting to `.main` gives natural UI semantics in production, but a serial utility queue keeps tests off the main thread and honest.
- **`flush` sets `workItem = nil` before performing** — the action runs synchronously on the caller's thread; the pending delayed execution is already consumed. If the action itself calls `call`, that becomes a new pending item and won't be clobbered.
- **Not thread-safe across *different* queues** — the lock protects the state machine, but asyncAfter scheduling on a specific queue means cross-queue ordering isn't guaranteed. For most UI and API call sites, a single debouncer-per-source pattern is correct.

```go
package ratelimit

import (
	"sync"
	"time"
)

type TokenBucket struct {
	mu       sync.Mutex
	capacity float64
	tokens   float64
	rate     float64 // tokens per second
	last     time.Time
}

func NewTokenBucket(capacity, rate float64) *TokenBucket {
	return &TokenBucket{
		capacity: capacity,
		tokens:   capacity,
		rate:     rate,
		last:     time.Now(),
	}
}

func (b *TokenBucket) Allow() bool {
	b.mu.Lock()
	defer b.mu.Unlock()

	now := time.Now()
	elapsed := now.Sub(b.last).Seconds()
	b.last = now

	b.tokens += elapsed * b.rate
	if b.tokens > b.capacity {
		b.tokens = b.capacity
	}

	if b.tokens >= 1 {
		b.tokens--
		return true
	}
	return false
}
```

Two ways this can fail under load:

1. **Mutex contention**: Every `Allow()` call takes the same mutex. At high concurrency, requests serialize on that lock, so latency rises and throughput can collapse due to contention/context switching. Sharding or per-key limiters can help.

2. **Burstiness overloads downstream**: The bucket can refill up to `capacity`, so after an idle period it may admit a large burst instantly. Even if the average rate is correct, that burst can overwhelm a downstream service. Smaller capacity or smoothing/leaky-bucket behavior mitigates this.

```kotlin
import java.util.Locale
import java.util.PriorityQueue

data class WordFrequency(val word: String, val count: Int)

// Compiled once: don't rebuild the regex on every service call.
// This treats runs of Unicode letters/numbers as one word.
private val WORD_PATTERN = Regex("[\\p{L}\\p{N}]+")

// Desired final order: most frequent first, then alphabetically for deterministic ties.
private val BEST_FIRST: Comparator<WordFrequency> =
    compareByDescending<WordFrequency> { it.count }
        .thenBy { it.word }

// Heap order: the "worst" candidate is at the head, so it can be evicted.
// Worst = lowest count, and for equal counts, lexicographically largest word.
private val WORST_FIRST: Comparator<WordFrequency> =
    compareBy<WordFrequency> { it.count }
        .thenByDescending { it.word }

/**
 * Returns the [k] most frequent words in [text].
 *
 * Ordering:
 *  - count descending
 *  - word ascending when counts tie
 *
 * Complexity:
 *  - O(N + U log k) time, where N is text length and U is unique words
 *  - O(U + k) memory
 */
fun topKFrequentWords(text: String, k: Int): List<WordFrequency> {
    if (k <= 0) return emptyList()

    val counts = HashMap<String, Int>()

    for (match in WORD_PATTERN.findAll(text)) {
        // Locale.ROOT makes case folding deterministic across server locales.
        // Do NOT use Locale.getDefault() unless you explicitly want locale-specific
        // behavior, e.g. Turkish I/İ rules.
        val word = match.value.lowercase(Locale.ROOT)
        counts[word] = (counts[word] ?: 0) + 1
    }

    if (counts.isEmpty()) return emptyList()

    val heapSize = minOf(k, counts.size)
    val heap = PriorityQueue(heapSize, WORST_FIRST)

    for ((word, count) in counts) {
        val candidate = WordFrequency(word, count)

        if (heap.size < heapSize) {
            heap.add(candidate)
        } else if (BEST_FIRST.compare(candidate, heap.peek()) < 0) {
            // candidate is better than the current worst element
            heap.poll()
            heap.add(candidate)
        }
    }

    return heap.toList().sortedWith(BEST_FIRST)
}
```

### Tricky parts worth documenting

1. **Tokenization is deliberately simple.**
   `[\\p{L}\\p{N}]+` treats runs of letters/numbers as words. It will not split contractions, hyphenated words, camelCase, emoji, or language-specific compounds. For production NLP/search, use ICU `BreakIterator` or a language-aware tokenizer.

2. **Case folding is locale-sensitive.**
   `lowercase(Locale.ROOT)` avoids surprises like Turkish `I`/`İ`. If your product needs locale-aware behavior, pass a `Locale` explicitly.

3. **Tie-breaking must be deterministic.**
   If two words have the same count, order them alphabetically. Otherwise results can vary between runs and make tests/caching unstable. If you need “first occurrence wins” instead, track the first index in a `LinkedHashMap` and use it as the tie-breaker.

4. **Top-k selection uses a min-heap.**
   The heap keeps only the best `k` candidates. The head is the worst item currently kept, so a better candidate can replace it in `O(log k)`. This is better than sorting all unique words when `k` is small.

5. **Large inputs need streaming.**
   This function takes a full `String`. If your service receives huge documents, read and tokenize incrementally from a `Reader`/`BufferedReader` instead of loading everything into memory.

