Assuming PostgreSQL/PLpgSQL (which supports 2D arrays), here’s an in-place 90° clockwise rotation:

```sql
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION rotate_matrix_90(INOUT m int[][])
RETURNS int[][] AS $$
DECLARE
  n      int := array_length(m, 1);
  first  int;
  last   int;
  j      int;
  offset int;
  top    int;
BEGIN
  IF n IS NULL OR n <> array_length(m, 2) THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'Matrix must be NxN';
  END IF;

  -- Rotate layer by layer, clockwise, in place
  FOR first IN 1 .. n / 2 LOOP
    last := n - first + 1;

    FOR j IN first .. last - 1 LOOP
      offset := j - first;

      top := m[first][j];

      m[first][j]                 := m[last - offset][first];
      m[last - offset][first]     := m[last][last - offset];
      m[last][last - offset]      := m[j][last];
      m[j][last]                  := top;
    END LOOP;
  END LOOP;
END;
$$ LANGUAGE plpgsql IMMUTABLE;
```

Usage example:

```sql
SELECT rotate_matrix_90(
  ARRAY[
    [1, 2, 3],
    [4, 5, 6],
    [7, 8, 9]
  ]
) AS rotated;
```

Result:

```text
{{7,4,1},{8,5,2},{9,6,3}}
```

That rotates the matrix 90 degrees clockwise in place within the function parameter.

