This Rust code has a bug. Find it, explain it, and fix it:

```
fn largest(v: &Vec<i32>) -> &i32 {
    let mut m = &v[0];
    for x in v { if x > m { m = x; } }
    m
}
// called with an empty vec in production
```

The bug is here:

```rust
let mut m = &v[0];
```

If `v` is empty, `v[0]` panics with an index-out-of-bounds error. Since the comment says it is called with an empty vector in production, this is a real crash.

Also, because the function returns `&i32`, there is no way to represent “there is no largest element” unless you panic or change the return type.

A good fix is to return `Option<&i32>`:

```rust
fn largest(v: &[i32]) -> Option<&i32> {
    v.iter().max()
}
```

Or equivalently, manually:

```rust
fn largest(v: &[i32]) -> Option<&i32> {
    let mut m = v.first()?;

    for x in v {
        if x > m {
            m = x;
        }
    }

    Some(m)
}
```

Then callers must handle the empty case:

```rust
match largest(&v) {
    Some(max) => println!("largest = {max}"),
    None => println!("empty vector"),
}
```

If the function must always return `&i32`, then it cannot be safely called with an empty vector; it would need to panic deliberately or require a non-empty input type. But for production code, returning `Option<&i32>` is the correct Rust approach.

