This C++ code has a bug. Find it, explain it, and fix it:

```
std::vector<int> v = {1, 2, 3, 4};
for (auto it = v.begin(); it != v.end(); ++it) {
    if (*it % 2 == 0) v.erase(it);
}
```

The bug is **iterator invalidation** caused by calling `v.erase(it)` inside a loop.

`std::vector::erase` invalidates the iterator passed to it, and also invalidates all iterators after the erased element. The loop then does `++it` on that invalidated iterator, which is undefined behavior.

There is also a logic problem: even if incrementing the invalid iterator happened to “work,” erasing an element shifts the following elements left, so `++it` would skip the element that moved into the erased position.

Bad:

```cpp
for (auto it = v.begin(); it != v.end(); ++it) {
    if (*it % 2 == 0) v.erase(it);
}
```

Fix by using the iterator returned by `erase`, which points to the next element, and only incrementing when no erase happened:

```cpp
for (auto it = v.begin(); it != v.end(); ) {
    if (*it % 2 == 0) {
        it = v.erase(it);
    } else {
        ++it;
    }
}
```

Or, more idiomatically, use the erase-remove idiom:

```cpp
#include <algorithm>

v.erase(
    std::remove_if(v.begin(), v.end(), [](int x) {
        return x % 2 == 0;
    }),
    v.end()
);
```

Both correct versions remove all even numbers: `{1, 3}`.

