This CUDA C++ code has a bug. Find it, explain it, and fix it:

```
__global__ void add(float *a, float *b, float *c, int n) {
    int i = threadIdx.x;
    c[i] = a[i] + b[i];
}
// launched as add<<<n/256, 256>>>(a, b, c, n);
```

The bug is that the kernel only uses `threadIdx.x` for the global index:

```cpp
int i = threadIdx.x;
```

That means every block computes indices `0..255`, because each block has 256 threads. If you launch `n/256` blocks, all blocks write to the same first 256 elements of `c`, causing race conditions and leaving most of `c` unwritten.

You need to include `blockIdx.x` and `blockDim.x`:

```cpp
int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;
```

Also, if `n` is not an exact multiple of 256, you need a bounds check, and you should launch enough blocks to cover all elements.

Fixed kernel:

```cpp
__global__ void add(float *a, float *b, float *c, int n) {
    int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;
    if (i < n) {
        c[i] = a[i] + b[i];
    }
}
```

Correct launch:

```cpp
int threads = 256;
int blocks = (n + threads - 1) / threads;
add<<<blocks, threads>>>(a, b, c, n);
```

So the key fix is:

```cpp
int i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;
```

plus the `if (i < n)` guard when `n` may not be divisible by the block size.

