Review this TypeScript snippet like a senior engineer. What breaks and when?

```
function groupBy<T>(xs: T[], key: keyof T) {
  return xs.reduce((acc, x) => {
    (acc[x[key]] ||= []).push(x);
    return acc;
  }, {});
}
```

This snippet is a classic “works for simple string keys, then explodes in real data” implementation.

```ts
function groupBy<T>(xs: T[], key: keyof T) {
  return xs.reduce((acc, x) => {
    (acc[x[key]] ||= []).push(x);
    return acc;
  }, {});
}
```

## What breaks

### 1. It doesn’t type-check under `strict`

The accumulator starts as `{}`, so `acc` is inferred as `{}`. Then:

```ts
acc[x[key]]
```

`x[key]` has type `T[keyof T]`, which TypeScript does not know is a valid property key. It can be an object, boolean, etc. So you get errors like:

- “Element implicitly has an `any` type because expression of type `T[keyof T]` can’t be used to index type `{}`”
- Or “Type `T[keyof T]` cannot be used as an index type”

The return type is also effectively `{}` or `any`, not `Record<K, T[]>`.

If `noImplicitAny` is off, it may compile, but it will silently produce wrong results.

### 2. Plain `{}` is unsafe for arbitrary keys

Because `{}` inherits from `Object.prototype`, keys like `"__proto__"`, `"constructor"`, `"toString"`, etc. already exist and are truthy.

Example:

```ts
groupBy([{ k: "__proto__" }, { k: "__proto__" }], "k");
```

Here `acc["__proto__"]` is `Object.prototype`, not an array. `||=` sees a truthy value, so it does not initialize `[]`. Then `.push` is called on `Object.prototype` and throws:

```txt
TypeError: acc[x[key]].push is not a function
```

This is also a prototype-pollution-shaped footgun.

### 3. Keys are coerced to property keys

Object property keys are strings or symbols. So:

```ts
groupBy([{ k: 1 }, { k: "1" }], "k");
```

groups `1` and `"1"` together.

Objects collapse to `"[object Object]"`:

```ts
groupBy([{ k: {} }, { k: {} }], "k");
```

Both end up under `"[object Object]"`.

`undefined` and `null` become `"undefined"` and `"null"`.

### 4. `keyof T` is too permissive

`keyof T` allows methods, symbol keys, etc. It does not guarantee `T[key]` is a valid grouping key. Also if `x` is `null`/`undefined` at runtime, `x[key]` throws.

## Better version

If you want string/number/symbol keys and a prototype-safe object:

```ts
function groupBy<T, K extends PropertyKey>(
  xs: T[],
  getKey: (x: T) => K
): Record<K, T[]> {
  const acc = Object.create(null) as Record<K, T[]>;

  for (const x of xs) {
    const k = getKey(x);
    (acc[k] ??= []).push(x);
  }

  return acc;
}
```

Usage:

```ts
groupBy(users, u => u.role);
```

If keys can be objects, or you want no coercion/collision surprises, use `Map`:

```ts
function groupBy<T, K>(
  xs: T[],
  getKey: (x: T) => K
): Map<K, T[]> {
  const acc = new Map<K, T[]>();

  for (const x of xs) {
    const k = getKey(x);
    const group = acc.get(k);

    if (group) {
      group.push(x);
    } else {
      acc.set(k, [x]);
    }
  }

  return acc;
}
```

Use `Map` when identity matters. Use `Object.create(null)` + `Record` only when keys are guaranteed to be `PropertyKey` and you actually want JS property-key coercion semantics.

